{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786993884,"nonce":"01Ih98314n6glytpgbbd+dNPmOQ=","title":"`alerts.ack_state` claims an alert is acknowledged when the firing it referred to has closed","message":"**What is wrong.** An ack is a statement about **one firing**. `alerts.ack_state`\n(`db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:42`) projects the current occurrence's ack onto an entity\nthat outlives the firing, so it keeps asserting a claim whose subject has ended.\n\n**The demonstration.** The projection is written from the occurrence transition\n(`internal/alerts/repository/alert.go:921`, `ack_state = p.ack_state`), so it tracks the\ncurrent occurrence and nothing else:\n\n```\n10:00  fires   → occurrence 7 opens, human acks → alerts.ack_state = 'acked'\n10:05  resolves → occurrence 7 closes\n10:05 … 14:00   alerts.state      = 'resolved'   honest, that IS its state\n                alerts.ack_state  = 'acked'      a claim about a closed episode\n14:00  fires again → occurrence 8, ack_state defaults 'unacked' → flips back\n```\n\n`occ_ackorder_ck CHECK (acked_at IS NULL OR acked_at \u003e= started_at)` already states that an\nack cannot exist without an episode. The projection is the only place that contradicts it.\n\nIts two remaining consumers pull opposite ways. `alert.go:673` is the `?ack=` list filter and\n**no index on `alerts` includes `ack_state`** — the ten indexes cover `state`, `last_seen_at`,\n`alertname`, `namespace`, `severity`, `source_fingerprint` and `snoozed_until`, so `?ack=acked`\nscans `(org_id, last_seen_at DESC, id DESC)` and discards off the heap, which degrades as the\ntable grows because acked alerts are the minority. `alert.go:843` is\n`count(*) FILTER (WHERE ack_state = 'acked') AS acked` in the grouped roll-up, where it is the\nonly counter that is not a property of the alert — `firing`/`suppressed`/`resolved`/`expired`\nare the current episode's state, `flapping` and `snoozed` are alert-scoped, `acked` is not.\n\nDisplay does not need it: `web/src/features/alerts/filters.ts:318` records that\n`current_occurrence` \"is what makes the row show ack state\", so the row already expands the\nepisode.\n\n**Why it matters.** The list of unacknowledged things is the surface people triage from, and\nits trustworthiness is the whole value of ack. A column that can assert \"acknowledged\" about a\nfiring that ended is the mechanism by which a September firing arrives pre-acknowledged because\nsomebody acked in March — never entering anyone's queue, never looked at. ADR 0013 builds the\nalert-first story on acknowledge being one of exactly two inbound human verbs; an ack that\nsilently carries across episodes is that verb lying. Ack is also the only one of the three axes\nwith no answer at all once the episode ends, unlike `state`, which always has one — which is\nwhy `state` stays projected and this does not.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:42, 57, 112`;\n`internal/alerts/repository/alert.go:42, 60, 71, 131, 673, 843, 921, 929`;\n`internal/notification/repository/snapshot.go:110, 129, 649`;\n`internal/notification/service/view.go:359`;\n`web/src/features/alerts/filters.ts:75, 99, 139, 156, 200-217, 293`.\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** `alerts.ack_state` does not exist. The `?ack=` filter and its URL parameter are\ngone from the API and the UI, and the ack filter and count live on the Case surface instead.\nThe roll-up drops its `acked` column. `notification/repository/snapshot.go` reads per-member\nack from the member's own case rather than `a.ack_state` — `alert_group_members` already\ncarries the occurrence id, so the member row *is* a case, which is both simpler and more\ncorrect. `reminders.go:52` needs no change; it already reads `o.ack_state` off the occurrence.\nAn arch test refuses the reintroduction of an ack column on `alerts`.\n\nSequencing matters: redirect the notification reads before dropping the column. A cross-module\nSQL read has no import and no compiler error to break.","files":null}]}